WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.640
Snoopy's Silly Sports Spectacular!

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.980
There's more fun here than a month of Sunday comics!

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:26.960
Nostalgia! And welcome to NEStalgia!

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:31.880
A chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.900
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.280
And I'm Joe.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.620
Is that a diss at the Peanuts?

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.020
I don't even know where we are right now.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.900
There's more fun here than a month of Sunday comics?

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.840
Like, don't bother reading the stuff Charles Schultz is writing because, like, the game is more fun?

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.520
I can read it that way.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.140
This is Charles Schultz's, like, angry, jealous brother who's like,

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.320
I made an even better Snoopy thing.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.560
You have to understand how I got to that conclusion of the back of the box

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:19.840
And it writes this whole thing about, like, all the different silly sports and what they're gonna be doing and everything.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:27.780
And then it just puts at the very bottom, which is what I chose for the back of the box, it just puts in parentheses, alternative, dot, dot, dot.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.720
There's more fun here than a month of Sunday comics.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.160
It's like... Alternative?

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.540
Like, that's an alternative thing to read?

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.640
Like, they left in the notes about the copy?

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.880
Yeah, they, like, left in the notes.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.600
Exactly. Wow, that's weird.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.460
That's funny. Also, I never knew Dusty was his brother.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.780
I thought Dusty was, like, his old uncle or something.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.820
I didn't even know there was, like, another looking Snoopy character.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.520
I didn't know that was a thing.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.700
I thought there'd be more peanuts in this, but I guess they did just call it Snoopy.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.000
I don't know why they're in Italy.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.800
Can we, like, just do, like, a post-mortem here?

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:08.580
Yeah, well, I have a question of why do they fly west from New York to Italy?

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.400
Doesn't that take way longer?

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:12.620
And why are both cities in the clouds?

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:14.900
That was my secondary concern.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:20.060
Well, if they're in the clouds, right, to just, just as everybody's on the same page, Snoopy's

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:24.980
Silly Sports Spectacular takes place in apparently not just Italy, but Greece.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.960
I don't think the Greece one, like, has any monuments, like how the Italy has, like, the

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:33.580
Coliseum and stuff, but they insist that he also went to Greece in this, uh, in this trip.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:40.400
And the reason why that's confusing is because they're showing at the very beginning of the

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:49.220
game the Statue of Liberty and the USA and Snoopy flying from that cloud to a cloud that has

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.640
the Coliseum and Italy and everything.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.100
And it's like, okay, cool. I get it.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:03:01.100
Um, he's, that they're like letting it known in a silly, spectacular way that, um, that he

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.260
is in, that he's competing in Rome.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:09.260
Right. But like, why, why does he have to compete in Italy?

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.960
Like this, the Silly Sports Spectacular isn't a franchisable thing.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:19.020
It's not something that happens like, oh, don't forget, uh, every August, everybody tunes

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.720
into the Silly Sports Spectacular in Pisa.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.420
Yeah. This is exactly, here's where I'm thinking this happened.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.900
There was just a bunch of miscommunications, uh, while they were actually designing the game.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.880
The first little mini game that got designed was the pizza balance thing.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.460
And they're like, oh, they'll put the, put the leading tower of pizza behind it.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:46.100
And then whoever was next, like, you know, the next guy that had to design a game for

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:52.360
this was like, okay, so this, this has to be something that's all in Italy and it surrounds.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:58.240
So, and then that just sort of, that idea just sort of took off and you end up with a fully

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:05.120
Italian centric slash, I guess, some Greek, uh, Snoopy game.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:06.540
I think that's how it happened.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.320
That everyone was asking for.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:10.140
Yeah. That's a great question, Joe.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.940
Is anybody asking for this?

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:19.160
I think yes, because look, like it or not, every, uh, Halloween, Thanksgiving and Christmas,

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:24.800
the peanuts resurrect from the dead and become part of ABC television.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.300
Oh yeah. And so they, they are, you know, they are incredibly popular.

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.700
I'm not saying that the Snoopy IP was about anyone's asking for.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.780
It's like the, everything around it was, is just kind of randomness.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:37.960
Not in a bad way.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:42.440
I'm just, it just, it was just a weird way that this came up, like came to be.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:47.660
I'd argue that there weren't too many people asking for a Snoopy video game too, but that's just me.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:55.280
I just don't think they were asking for this kind of Snoopy video game because look, Snoopy is without a doubt the coolest peanut character, right?

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.480
Like who's even second to Snoopy?

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.160
Minus is second, but yeah, Snoopy's.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.580
Okay, good. So at least you're agreeing Snoopy's first.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:12.940
And maybe that says more about the characters Charles Schultz created than anything because, uh, the dog is the coolest character, um, on the show, not the actual humans who talk and have personalities.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.580
I like the dog.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:16.540
Woodstock's up there.

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:17.940
I like, I like Snoopy.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:19.840
Did you know Snoopy had a brother?

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:21.780
No. Okay.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.980
Me neither. But, uh, he's an asshole in this game.

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:33.060
I mean, he seems to be, uh, like more of victim of preferential treatment from the bird.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:34.640
Uh, that's all I know.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:36.080
Wait, how so?

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.840
The bird will like, you'll push him.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.240
Well, he pushes whoever's losing.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:43.540
Oh. It's not just him.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:44.780
Yeah. Okay.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.780
I never, I guess I never lost at that bad.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:50.840
I never won the sack, the sack race.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:55.540
Um, but watching other people play the other guys getting pushed.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:06:00.100
So. Yeah. I, I, I, I once again played two player on this.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.740
So one of us was always losing, which is how that works.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:04.200
I don't know why I'm saying that.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.620
Two observations.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.100
First off, Sean, nobody else has ever played this game.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:09.700
You're watching computers play this.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:15.340
Okay. And then, um, following up on that for Joe's thing.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:20.100
Joe, you know, it is a good point that you played with another person, but also Snoopy

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:25.220
went all the way to Italy to do the silly sports spectacular, or as it's referred to in

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:29.160
the manual, just the international games, which is not like the Olympics.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.820
It's totally its own thing.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:34.860
Uh, and he went all the way there just to compete against his brother.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:39.820
They could have done this in fucking Charlie Brown's backyard, but instead they went, they

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:44.440
expensed a trip on Charles Schultz's dime to go to Italy for this.

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:49.720
Well, you only can find a gondola to sumo wrestle on in Venice.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.160
Yeah. Cause they, you're right.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.820
What are they wrestling? They must be, they're just pushing each other.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:55.160
Sumo wrestling is being nice.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:56.520
They're just pushing each other.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:00.340
And again, uh, his brother spikes just being a dick here.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:02.000
By putting up a fight.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:03.600
Like, do you have a problem with him?

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:06.280
Like trying to compete with the golden child?

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:10.060
Like does, does Snoopy the golden child have to win everything?

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:12.200
When you're the, when you're playing a Snoopy.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:16.340
Yes. If I was given the choice to play as spike, I would have, I would have picked spike over

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:18.340
Snoopy just to have the underdog.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:20.740
Get it. Uh, win.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:24.160
All right. At least we can agree on that.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:28.020
All right. So forget about the locations cause that's tripping us up too much.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:33.720
Now we need to talk about, uh, the actual game and the silly sports.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:43.000
Uh, the silly sports are boot throwing, pizza, balancing, sack racing, pogo, river jump, and

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:48.780
overboarding. And I have a lot of thoughts of which we'll get to, but the biggest one would just be that,

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:53.720
uh, how did like the pogo stick get shafted so hard here?

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:57.880
That's like, that could be a real sport that involves a real piece of equipment.

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:02.340
The other ones are literally games that somebody just made up that day.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:06.000
Boot throwing is just taking a boot and swing.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:10.800
It's no different than like doing a hammer throw or, um, javalin or whatever.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:14.620
No, I know, but they're just making it a boot instead and it's funny and it's silly.

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:17.020
And the way you throw the boot is funny and silly, blah, blah, blah.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:24.020
I get it. But pogo ing, if that's a verb, is just something that like people actually do.

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:25.820
And now it's a silly sport.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:30.400
That would be like if it was boot throwing, pizza, balancing, sack, racing, river jump,

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:33.920
and karate, you know, it's like, oh, so now karate's silly.

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:40.640
In, in, in the defense of the decision-making here, I'd say that no one looks like,

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:44.160
like normal on a pogo stick, on a pogo stick.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:46.000
Like it's, it's pretty ridiculous in itself.

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:50.140
I was going to say, regardless of, you know, I'm sure there's very serious pogo stickers

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:54.380
out there. This game is not the first thing to ever consider pogo sticks silly.

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:01.340
So let's just, let's go through them one by one.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:05.140
I gave you a boot throwing and what the equivalents are.

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:10.440
Uh, you could talk about the game mechanics or you can just talk about the silliness factor

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:11.340
of the sport.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:13.100
You have at it. You have your pick.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.940
So Sean, take us through your thoughts on boot throwing.

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:21.320
Uh, yeah, it's, it's pretty much just, I don't know why they selected boot.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:23.540
I don't know why that's like a boot that you're throwing.

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:28.540
I'm sure there's any number of silly items that you could, you could throw.

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:32.060
Um, and they just selected boot.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:35.980
I mean, I guess what's like a shot put or I guess, yeah, hammer throw, like you said.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:40.140
Um, it, you know, you just spin your little D pad in a circle.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:46.700
You try and, you try and, uh, you try and stop it at the optimal angle.

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:49.640
And then, I don't know, the highest I could get was 30.

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:53.080
Um, but that was fine.

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:57.140
That was fine. I think they could have picked a sillier, a sillier item though.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:59.540
If we're going for maximum silliness.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:07.580
Yeah. I think that this one also for me might be the, the most forgettable or basic.

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:10.180
It's like, it's like a very quick, like one thing.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:16.580
It's yeah. The moment of release, we tried it a couple of times and we would either at first get like zero,

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:18.460
or one cause we were bad at it.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:22.560
And then we both just kept getting 30, which I'm wondering if that's the max.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:23.860
I feel like that's the max.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:26.780
Yeah. So it felt like that one was like, okay, we did it.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:32.360
And not to say that like that other games in this, like you're going to keep coming back

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:33.960
to for, for decades to come.

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:38.000
But like after, after boot throwing, I was like, I don't ever need to do that again.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.240
Sean, you said you would have liked to have seen a sillier item than a boot.

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:45.460
Yeah. I mean, why a boot?

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:46.460
What else?

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:48.340
What do you got? I don't know.

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:51.320
Pitch it to us. How about a calzone?

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:52.570
A, okay.

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:54.820
Yeah. Calzone is one.

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:57.940
Um, a bag of lettuce.

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:03.840
Um, calzone sounds right because we're keeping it at least in the Italian vein, but try not

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:05.280
to do it. Well, a boot has nothing to do with Italy.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:07.320
Well, no, it, yes.

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:08.500
It's because of Italy.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:10.760
It's a boot. Guys, I just figured it out.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:12.880
Italy is in the shape of a boot.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:14.860
Oh, it is in the shape of a boot.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.620
Oh, wow. I can't wait for you to do this for every other game, Joe.

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:21.120
And you can just figure it out every time.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:25.240
Yep. Well, the next one needs no help because it's the only reason they went to Italy.

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:29.960
And that is for the pizza balancing competition, which makes sense because they're in Pisa.

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:37.080
You see the leaning tower and you yourself are carrying, carrying, carrying a leaning tower

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:44.440
of pizzas, uh, which you have to, like, manage balancing while also moving with these pizzas.

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:50.300
And, uh, of course it would be cool if they had any kind of actual physics and, and, uh,

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:52.340
movement to it. Don't go too fast.

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:54.000
It's don't go too fast.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:59.920
And also it's just that the pizzas all just move like, um, kind of like a vertical slinky

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:00.940
would move.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:05.560
And if they just move a little too far off to the sides and you don't pull back on the

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:09.220
controller, uh, the, the top pizzas will fall over.

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:14.160
And so you want to have almost all your pizzas still there and you want to do it somewhat fast.

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:15.640
And that's how you get the most amount of points.

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:24.220
All this to say, I did appreciate the actual, like feel of using the D pad to reign in my pizzas.

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:25.680
I don't know if it worked at all.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:32.440
Like, I think sometimes they would fall regardless, but I felt the invisible touch of pulling back.

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:39.140
That would be the, um, the left button on my D pad, pushing that to kind of help pull the pizzas back,

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:40.780
whether or not it actually did anything.

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:42.860
Yeah. I liked the, the look of it.

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:48.440
Uh, like it, well, each, each pizza while way too small to be considered pizzas.

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:53.920
Um, the fact that they're all like individually animated, I thought that was like,

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:55.260
Ooh, that looks cool. Yeah.

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:57.420
They're, they're personal pan pizzas for sure.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:04.040
But I, uh, I will defend a little bit the, uh, the kind of movement of it where I, I did

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:09.280
over time, like realize that there is like a rhythm you have to get into to go a little

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:14.960
faster. You can't go too fast still, but like at first we were doing like one step at a time, but

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:18.300
like there's a specific rhythm that where you have to kind of do the risk reward thing

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:24.780
of like, how fast do I want to go versus how, you know, safe do I want to be?

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:27.460
Uh, so like it did, it was different.

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:31.240
It's like, we haven't really had anything that's like kind of the opposite of most games

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:33.380
where it's like, be really careful with what you do.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:35.680
So like, I appreciated what it was trying to do.

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:39.700
And there is a balance in the controls schemes for almost all of these, right?

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:43.480
Like the, the boot throwing that we, you know, we didn't, we chose not to talk about the

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:50.120
gameplay, but you spin your D pad around to rev up Snoopy's arm and everything.

00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:55.300
And then you have to make sure that, you know, you're throwing, uh, the boot while it's

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:56.160
in forward momentum.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:00.440
Like you can theoretically be going, moving it really fast, but then just throwing the boot

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:01.920
backwards and it going nowhere.

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:03.980
Oh yeah. It did, it did a lot of that too.

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:09.840
Yeah. So, I mean, you know, these games are well designed for, for what they are.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:14.020
I'm not trying to rush through the sports and not go in depth enough.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:19.000
I think it's just worth talking about, um, each of the silly sports and making sure that

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:20.320
we covered that all in this episode.

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:25.200
So thinking about that, we're moving on to sack racing, which unfortunately now Joe has

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:29.260
to explain what sack racing has to do with, uh, Italy.

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:31.360
Yeah. I've been thinking about it this whole time.

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:34.940
And I'm sure it'll come to me while we're, uh, while we're in the middle of talking about

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:41.180
it. Great. So in the sack racing, this is, uh, the one that, uh, Sean was mentioning Woodstock is

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:45.080
favoring. Who's ever losing because Woodstock is just kind of hanging out.

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:46.100
He gives you a little push.

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:49.980
Makes you wonder why Woodstock in general just isn't helping with all the games then.

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:54.840
Like, why did he decide it's okay to cheat in the sack thing, but he's not like able to

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:58.800
catch one of your pizzas as they're falling and lift it back up onto the top.

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:00.140
Uh, you know, go figure.

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:05.360
I guess he didn't want to help there, but in the sack race, you're just, um, hopping

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:10.260
along and using the D pad to kind of maneuver yourself around the street where there are

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:15.600
these, uh, I guess either potholes or sewer caps or whatever that are in your way.

